-- title:
--   The actual matrices $A_f$, $B_f$, $D_f=A_{\Phi(f)}$, the discriminant $\Delta_f$, projective order, and projective groups in $\mathrm{PGL}_2(\mathbb C)$
-- statement:
--   Let $f=[f_0,f_1,f_2,f_3]$ be a symmetric ternary signature, and write $h=[w,x,y,z]$ for a generic one. A straddled gadget has a row index (the bit on its dangling left port) and a column index (the bit on its dangling right port), both ordered $0,1$ (p. 12).
--
--   1. The **first actual matrix** is $A_h(i,j)=h(i,j,j)$ (p. 13):
--   $$A_f=\begin{pmatrix}f_0&f_2\\ f_1&f_3\end{pmatrix},\qquad A_{[1,a,b,c]}=\begin{pmatrix}1&b\\ a&c\end{pmatrix}.$$
--   2. The **second actual matrix** is $B_h(i,j)=\sum_t h(i,j,t)\,h(j,t,t)$ (p. 13):
--   $$B_h=\begin{pmatrix}w^2+xy&x^2+yz\\ wx+y^2&xy+z^2\end{pmatrix}.$$
--   3. The **ternary construction** is $\Phi(h)(x_1,x_2,x_3)=\sum_{s,t}\prod_j h(x_j,s,t)$ (p. 14), i.e. $\Phi(h)=[w^3+2x^3+y^3,\ w^2x+2x^2y+y^2z,\ wx^2+2xy^2+yz^2,\ x^3+2y^3+z^3]$. The **third actual matrix** is $D_f=A_{\Phi(f)}$.
--   4. The **discriminant** is $\Delta_f=(f_0-f_3)^2+4f_1f_2$ (p. 24); for $f=[1,a,b,c]$ it is $(1-c)^2+4ab$.
--   5. A $2\times2$ matrix $M$ has **projective order** $n$ if $n$ is the least positive integer for which $M^n$ is a nonzero scalar matrix $cI$, $c\neq0$ (p. 8).
--   6. For an invertible $M$, $[M]$ is its class in $\mathrm{PGL}_2(\mathbb C)=\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb C)/\mathbb C^\times$, here the set of its nonzero scalar multiples (p. 41). For a set $S$ of matrices, $\langle [M]: M\in S\rangle$ is the projective image of the subgroup of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb C)$ generated by the invertible members of $S$. It is **infinite** if infinitely many projective classes occur among its elements.
--   7. Matrices act on column lines, $[u]\mapsto[Mu]$ (p. 41). A group **preserves a projective point** if some line $\mathbb Cu$, $u\neq0$, is mapped into itself by every element. It **preserves an unordered pair of projective points** if there are two distinct lines $\mathbb Cu\neq\mathbb Cw$ such that every element either maps each of them into itself or interchanges them.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Matrices are `Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ`, written row by row (`!![f 0, f 2; f 1, f 3]`). $B_f$, $\Phi$ and $D_f$ are the closed forms printed on pp. 13–14. The group of item 6 is `genGroup S`, the subgroup of `Matrix.GeneralLinearGroup (Fin 2) ℂ` generated by the units whose matrix lies in `S`. An element maps a line into itself exactly when it maps it onto itself, because it is invertible.
-- source:
--   Yin Liu, A Dichotomy for Cubic Bipartite Holant Problems with Complex Algebraic Weights, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256 f70906c3…), p. 8 (§1.5), pp. 12–14 (§§2.1–2.2), p. 24 (§4.1), p. 41 (§7.1)

import Mathlib

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace CubicHolant.Dichotomy

/-! # The actual matrices `A_f`, `B_f`, `D_f`, the ternary construction `Φ`, the discriminant `Δ_f`,
projective order and projective groups

Yin Liu, *A Dichotomy for Cubic Bipartite Holant Problems with Complex Algebraic Weights*, STOC 2027
submission (manuscript sha256 f70906c3…), §1.5 p. 8, §2.1–2.2 pp. 12–14, §4.1 p. 24, §7.1 p. 41.

Matrices are `Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ`; the row index is the left-port bit and the column index
the right-port bit (p. 12). A signature `[f₀, f₁, f₂, f₃]` is `f : Fin 4 → ℂ`. -/

/-- The normalized signature `[1, a, b, c]`. -/
def normSig (a b c : ℂ) : Fin 4 → ℂ :=
  ![1, a, b, c]

/-- The first actual matrix `A_f(i, j) = f(i, j, j)`: `A_f = ((f₀, f₂), (f₁, f₃))` (rows), p. 13 and
p. 24; for `[1, a, b, c]` it is `((1, b), (a, c))`. -/
def matA (f : Fin 4 → ℂ) : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ :=
  !![f 0, f 2; f 1, f 3]

/-- The second actual matrix `B_h(i, j) = Σ_t h(i, j, t) h(j, t, t)` (p. 13): for `h = [w, x, y, z]`,
`B_h = ((w² + xy, x² + yz), (wx + y², xy + z²))` (rows). -/
def matB (f : Fin 4 → ℂ) : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ :=
  !![f 0 ^ 2 + f 1 * f 2, f 1 ^ 2 + f 2 * f 3; f 0 * f 1 + f 2 ^ 2, f 1 * f 2 + f 3 ^ 2]

/-- The ternary construction `Φ(h)(x₁, x₂, x₃) = Σ_{s,t} ∏ⱼ h(xⱼ, s, t)` (p. 14): for
`h = [w, x, y, z]`, `Φ(h) = [w³ + 2x³ + y³, w²x + 2x²y + y²z, wx² + 2xy² + yz², x³ + 2y³ + z³]`. -/
def phiT (f : Fin 4 → ℂ) : Fin 4 → ℂ :=
  ![f 0 ^ 3 + 2 * f 1 ^ 3 + f 2 ^ 3,
    f 0 ^ 2 * f 1 + 2 * (f 1 ^ 2 * f 2) + f 2 ^ 2 * f 3,
    f 0 * f 1 ^ 2 + 2 * (f 1 * f 2 ^ 2) + f 2 * f 3 ^ 2,
    f 1 ^ 3 + 2 * f 2 ^ 3 + f 3 ^ 3]

/-- The third actual matrix `D_f = A_{Φ(f)}` (p. 14). -/
def matD (f : Fin 4 → ℂ) : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ :=
  matA (phiT f)

/-- The discriminant `Δ_f = (f₀ − f₃)² + 4 f₁ f₂` (p. 24), homogeneous; for `[1, a, b, c]` it is
`(1 − c)² + 4ab` (p. 8). -/
def discr (f : Fin 4 → ℂ) : ℂ :=
  (f 0 - f 3) ^ 2 + 4 * f 1 * f 2

/-- `N` is a nonzero scalar matrix `c · I`, `c ≠ 0`. -/
def IsNonzeroScalar (N : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ) : Prop :=
  ∃ c : ℂ, c ≠ 0 ∧ N = c • (1 : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ)

/-- `M` has **projective order** `n` (p. 8): `n` is the least positive integer for which `Mⁿ` is a
nonzero scalar matrix. -/
def IsProjOrder (M : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ) (n : ℕ) : Prop :=
  0 < n ∧ IsNonzeroScalar (M ^ n) ∧ ∀ m : ℕ, 0 < m → m < n → ¬ IsNonzeroScalar (M ^ m)

/-- The class `[M]` of `M` in `PGL₂(ℂ) = GL₂(ℂ)/ℂ^×` (p. 41), as the set of its nonzero scalar
multiples. -/
def projClass (M : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ) : Set (Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ) :=
  {N | ∃ c : ℂ, c ≠ 0 ∧ N = c • M}

/-- The subgroup of `GL₂(ℂ)` generated by the invertible matrices in `S`; its image in `PGL₂(ℂ)` is
the group `⟨[M] : M ∈ S⟩` generated by their projective classes. -/
def genGroup (S : Set (Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ)) :
    Subgroup (Matrix.GeneralLinearGroup (Fin 2) ℂ) :=
  Subgroup.closure {g | (g : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ) ∈ S}

/-- The projective image of `H ≤ GL₂(ℂ)` is **infinite**: infinitely many projective classes
occur among the elements of `H`. -/
def ProjInfinite (H : Subgroup (Matrix.GeneralLinearGroup (Fin 2) ℂ)) : Prop :=
  Set.Infinite ((fun g : Matrix.GeneralLinearGroup (Fin 2) ℂ =>
    projClass (g : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ)) '' (H : Set (Matrix.GeneralLinearGroup (Fin 2) ℂ)))

/-- The column `w` lies on the line `ℂu`. -/
def OnLine (u w : Fin 2 → ℂ) : Prop :=
  ∃ c : ℂ, w = c • u

/-- The projective image of `H` **preserves a projective point** (p. 41): some column line `ℂu`,
`u ≠ 0`, is mapped into itself by every element of `H` (matrices act on columns, `[u] ↦ [Mu]`). -/
def PreservesPoint (H : Subgroup (Matrix.GeneralLinearGroup (Fin 2) ℂ)) : Prop :=
  ∃ u : Fin 2 → ℂ, u ≠ 0 ∧
    ∀ g ∈ H, OnLine u ((g : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ).mulVec u)

/-- The projective image of `H` **preserves an unordered pair of projective points** (p. 41): there
are two distinct column lines `ℂu ≠ ℂw` such that every element of `H` maps the set `{ℂu, ℂw}` to
itself, fixing both lines or interchanging them. -/
def PreservesPair (H : Subgroup (Matrix.GeneralLinearGroup (Fin 2) ℂ)) : Prop :=
  ∃ u w : Fin 2 → ℂ, u ≠ 0 ∧ w ≠ 0 ∧ ¬ OnLine u w ∧
    ∀ g ∈ H,
      (OnLine u ((g : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ).mulVec u) ∧
          OnLine w ((g : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ).mulVec w)) ∨
        (OnLine w ((g : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ).mulVec u) ∧
          OnLine u ((g : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ).mulVec w))

end CubicHolant.Dichotomy


